\chapter{\texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}: Compile-Time Strings as Template Parameters}

MuDSL is built around a single recurring idea: the names, tags, patterns, and rule identifiers that shape a domain-specific language should be known at compile time. A grammar rule called \texttt{"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}"}, a token tag called \texttt{"\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"}, and a regular-expression pattern like \texttt{"\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}"} are not runtime configuration values but structural facts about the program. Carrying those facts through the type system lets the compiler optimize, check, and dispatch on them without any runtime cost.

The obstacle, of course, is that C++ has historically refused to accept a string literal as a non-type template parameter (NTTP). This chapter introduces \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}, the small literal class type that removes that obstacle. Every downstream chapter that writes something inside angle brackets — \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>}, \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>}, \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>}, \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>}, \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>}, \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}>}, and the PEG \texttt{a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} — depends on the type defined here.

\section{The Problem: Strings as Template Parameters}

Before C++20, the non-type template parameter space was narrow. A template could be parameterized by integral constants, enumerators, pointers, and references to objects with static storage duration. A string literal, however, is an array of \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r} with automatic storage duration, and arrays decay to pointers in most contexts. The obvious attempt fails to compile.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
template <const char *Tag> struct leaf;   // declaration

leaf<"num"> n;                             // error before C++20
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The compiler rejects this because \texttt{"\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"} does not designate an object with static storage duration in the sense the standard requires for a pointer NTTP. Even when a workaround using a reference to an array was employed, each distinct tag still required a separate named variable declared at namespace scope, which defeated the goal of writing self-documenting inline tags.

The consequence for a library like MuDSL would be severe. Instead of writing \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{})}, a user would have to declare \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{};} somewhere and then write \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{})}. The tag would no longer live at the point of use, grammar definitions would sprawl across declarations, and the pedagogical clarity that MuDSL is designed to deliver would evaporate. A type-level solution is needed.

The same obstacle blocks more than convenience. Many of MuDSL's compile-time mechanisms — pattern dispatch in \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>}, rule naming in the rewrite system, production naming in the PEG layer — rely on the compiler being able to distinguish one string from another at instantiation time. Without a string NTTP, each of those mechanisms would have to fall back on runtime comparison of \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} values, which forfeits inlining, type-level dispatch, and the guarantee that two grammatically identical tags denote the same specialization. The library's design depends on the tag being part of the type, not part of the data.

\section{C++20 Class-Type Non-Type Template Parameters}

C++20 relaxed the NTTP rules in a targeted way. A literal class type may now be used as a non-type template parameter provided that all of its non-static data members are of structural types, none are of reference type, and the class meets the usual literal-type requirements. Structural types include scalar types, lvalue reference types, and arrays of structural types. A class containing a plain \texttt{c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r} array therefore qualifies.

This is the door that \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} walks through. Because it is a literal class type whose only non-static data member is a \texttt{c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r} array, it is a valid NTTP. Each distinct string literal produces a distinct \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}>} value, and the compiler treats two \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} values with the same contents and length as the same template argument. The library can finally write \texttt{l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} and mean it.

The structural-type rule has a subtle but important consequence. Two template arguments of class type are considered to designate the same template parameter when their corresponding members have the same values. For \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}, that means the bytes of \texttt{d\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a} must match element by element. This is exactly the semantics a tag type should have: two nodes tagged \texttt{"\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"} are interchangeable regardless of where they were constructed.

\section{The \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}>} Definition}

The type is deliberately minimal. The entire definition fits in ten lines and depends only on \texttt{<\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}>} for \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}t}, \texttt{<\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}>} for \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}n}, and \texttt{<\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}>} for the \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} return type. Quoted verbatim from \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p}:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
template <std::size_t N> struct FixedString
{
  char data[N]{};
  constexpr FixedString (const char (&s)[N]) { std::copy_n (s, N, data); }
  constexpr std::string_view
  view () const
  {
    return { data, N - 1 };
  }
  constexpr bool operator== (const FixedString &) const = default;
};
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Every member is \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}, every operation is trivially copyable, and the type has no virtual functions, no base classes, and no reference members. The design is intentionally compatible with being stored in the compiler's internal representation of a template argument.

The single template parameter \texttt{N} is the length of the string including its null terminator. A \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}4\allowbreak{}>} therefore holds three visible characters plus a trailing \texttt{'\allowbreak{}\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}'}. This convention matches the declared type of a string literal in C++, which is \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}]} for an N-character literal, and it is what allows the constructor to deduce \texttt{N} directly from a literal argument.

The empty string is a valid special case. A \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}>} carries only the terminator and represents a zero-length view. It arises naturally in the library whenever a rule or suffix is intentionally left blank, and the structural-equality rules treat all such instances as the same specialization. No special-casing is required in the definition to support it.

\section{Storage and the Null Terminator}

The data member \texttt{c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}\{\allowbreak{}\}} is value-initialized, so every byte starts at zero. When the constructor runs, it copies all \texttt{N} bytes from the source array, terminator included, into \texttt{d\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a}. The trailing null is therefore always present, which makes \texttt{d\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a} safe to pass to any C API that expects a NUL-terminated string in addition to being usable through \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}.

The null terminator is not merely decorative. Because \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} returns a \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} of length \texttt{N\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}1}, the visible string never includes the terminator; but the raw \texttt{d\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a} array still ends in \texttt{'\allowbreak{}\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}'}, so \texttt{d\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a} can be used directly where a C-style string is required. This dual nature — a C string when you need one, a sized view when you do not — is a small but persistent convenience throughout the library.

The fact that \texttt{N} is part of the type is the central design trade-off. A \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}4\allowbreak{}>} and a \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}5\allowbreak{}>} are unrelated types as far as the type system is concerned, even if both happen to spell \texttt{"\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"} when padded. In practice this is not a limitation, because every literal in a program has a single, fixed length known to the compiler, and the deduction in the constructor picks the correct \texttt{N} automatically.

\section{The Constructor and Array-Reference Deduction}

The constructor's parameter type, \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}\&\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}]}, is a reference to an array of exactly \texttt{N} \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r}. This is the idiom that makes string-literal deduction work. When the constructor is called with \texttt{"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}"}, the literal has type \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}4\allowbreak{}]}, so \texttt{N} is deduced as 4 and the constructor receives a reference to that exact array. No decay to \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}*} occurs, and no length information is lost.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
constexpr dsl::FixedString a = "abc";   // N deduced as 4
static_assert(a.view() == "abc");
static_assert(a.view().size() == 3);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The body copies all \texttt{N} bytes with \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{})}. Because \texttt{N} includes the terminator, the copy carries the trailing \texttt{'\allowbreak{}\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}'} into \texttt{d\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a} as well. \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}n} is itself \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} in C++20, so the whole constructor is usable during constant evaluation.

The use of \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}n}, rather than a hand-rolled loop, is a deliberate readability choice. The standard algorithm is recognized by optimizers and produces the same code as a \texttt{m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}y} on trivially copyable types, while remaining explicitly \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}. The constructor body therefore stays a single statement, which keeps the definition short enough to inspect at a glance.

Because the parameter is a reference to an array of fixed size, the constructor rejects arguments whose size does not match the deduced \texttt{N}. This is not a runtime check; it is a compile-time consequence of the parameter type. Passing a \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} or a \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}*} simply does not match the constructor signature, which prevents accidental loss of length information.```cpp dsl::FixedString b("hello");            /\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{} N deduced as 6 /\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{} dsl::FixedString c(std::string("x")); /\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{} error: no matching constructor

