The constructor is not `explicit`, which allows the implicit conversion that takes place when a string literal is supplied as a template argument. Writing `dsl::leaf<"num">(42)` relies on the compiler implicitly constructing a `FixedString` from `"num"` at the template-argument position. This implicit conversion is the ergonomic keystone of the whole library.

## The `view()` Method

The `view()` member returns a `std::string_view` spanning the first `N - 1` characters of `data`. The length is one less than the array size precisely because the null terminator is excluded from the logical string. The returned view is a lightweight handle — a pointer and a length — and incurs no allocation.

```cpp
constexpr dsl::FixedString tag = "identifier";
constexpr std::string_view v = tag.view();
static_assert(v.size() == 10);
static_assert(v == "identifier");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The method is declared \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} so that it can be evaluated during constant expression. This matters because the library uses \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} inside \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} initializers, inside \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}t}, and inside the bodies of \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} functions such as \texttt{l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f} and \texttt{n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e}. Without \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}, the tag could not propagate into the type system at compile time.

The \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} return type was chosen over \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} deliberately. Returning a \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} would require dynamic allocation, which is not permitted during constant evaluation and would defeat the zero-overhead goal. A \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w}, by contrast, is trivially copyable and points directly into the \texttt{d\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a} array stored inside the \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} object, which lives as long as the template argument that holds it.

A consequence of this design is that the \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} returned by \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} is only valid for as long as the \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} from which it came. In MuDSL this is never a problem, because the \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} is a template argument and therefore lives for the duration of the program. Users who copy a \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} result into a local \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} variable should keep this lifetime in mind, but in ordinary usage the view is consumed immediately.

It is worth emphasizing that \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} never allocates and never throws. The \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} constructor it invokes is trivial, and \texttt{d\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a} is always a valid, NUL-terminated array. This makes \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} safe to call from \texttt{n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t} contexts and from code paths that must remain allocation-free, such as the hot paths of the matcher and the AST dumper.

\section{The Defaulted \texttt{o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}=}}

The final member is a defaulted equality operator. C++20's defaulted comparisons perform memberwise comparison, so two \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} objects compare equal if and only if every element of \texttt{d\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a} matches. Because \texttt{N} is part of the type, comparisons only occur between objects of the same length, and the comparison reduces to a simple byte-by-byte test over the array.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
constexpr dsl::FixedString x = "add";
constexpr dsl::FixedString y = "add";
constexpr dsl::FixedString z = "sub";
static_assert(x == y);
static_assert(!(x == z));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The \texttt{d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t} keyword ensures that the operator is both \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} and usable during constant evaluation. It also makes the comparison strong in the C++20 sense: there is no need to write a custom body, and the compiler is free to optimize the array comparison as it sees fit. The structural-equality semantics required for NTTPs align exactly with the semantics of this defaulted operator, so two \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} template arguments that compare equal also designate the same template specialization.

Because equality is \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}, tags can be compared at compile time inside \texttt{i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}, inside \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}t}, and inside any \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l} context. This is what enables the \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} dispatch machinery described in Chapter 8 to select among branches at compile time: each branch's key is a \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} (possibly wrapped in a \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n}), and the selection is a constant-expression comparison.

\section{All-\texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} Design}

Every operation \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} provides — construction, \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}, and equality — is \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}. The type is therefore usable in any constant-expression context, including the initialization of \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} data members, the arguments of \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}t}, the bodies of \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l} functions, and, crucially, the evaluation of template arguments. This uniformity is not an accident; it is the property that makes the type usable as an NTTP with runtime-accessible values.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
template <dsl::FixedString S> struct tag_of
{
  static constexpr std::string_view value = S.view ();
};

using add_tag = tag_of<"add">;
static_assert(add_tag::value == "add");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The fact that the bytes of a \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} template argument are readable at runtime, even though the argument itself is fixed at compile time, is what bridges the compile-time and runtime worlds. A function instantiated with \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} can call \texttt{T\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} at runtime and obtain a perfectly ordinary \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} that spells \texttt{"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}"}. This is how \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} ends up labelling its \texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} with a runtime string: the tag is a compile-time fact, but the label is read out at runtime through \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}.

This bridge is one-directional. A compile-time \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} can be projected into a runtime \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w}, but a runtime \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} cannot be lifted back into a \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} NTTP. MuDSL respects this asymmetry throughout: tags flow outward into runtime strings for display and matching, but never inward from runtime data into the type system. Recognizing this boundary is the key to using the library without fighting the language.

\section{How \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} Flows Into the Library}

\texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} is not a standalone utility; it is the connective tissue of the entire library. Almost every chapter that follows uses it in some form. The pattern is always the same: a string literal appears inside angle brackets, the compiler implicitly constructs a \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} from it, and the resulting NTTP parameterizes a class or function template.

The most direct uses are \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f} and \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e}, which build the leaf and inner nodes of an AST. Their signatures, quoted from \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p}, show the \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g} parameter in the leading position.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
template <FixedString Tag, typename T>
ASTNode leaf (T &&val);

template <FixedString Tag, typename... Children>
  requires (std::convertible_to<Children, ASTNode> && ...)
ASTNode node (Children &&...children);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Inside \texttt{l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f}, the tag is read out at runtime with \texttt{T\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} and stored as the \texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e}'s label. The value is streamed into a string and stored as the node's payload. The tag never becomes a runtime variable until \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} is called; before that, it is purely a compile-time fact carried in the type.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto n = dsl::leaf<"token">("identifier");
// n.tag() == "token", n payload == "identifier"
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}>} template, used by the compile-time regex engine described in Chapter 9, takes its regular-expression source as a \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}. The signature is \texttt{t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n}, and the body stores a \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}. The pattern string is therefore baked into the type at compile time and available to the matcher without any runtime construction.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
template <dsl::FixedString S> struct pattern
{
  static constexpr std::string_view value = S.view ();
  // ... matching machinery ...
};
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The custom-literal machinery in Chapter 17 uses \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} to name the suffix of a literal. The \texttt{l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t} factory and its \texttt{L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}y} carrier parameterize on \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x}.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
template <FixedString Suffix, typename F> struct LiteralEntry
{
  F handler;
  static constexpr std::string_view suffix = Suffix.view ();
  // ...
};

template <FixedString Suffix, typename F>
constexpr auto lit (F &&f);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

A usage such as \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}\{\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}*\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{};\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}\}\allowbreak{})} binds the suffix \texttt{"\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"} into the type at compile time, so that the parser can recognise the suffix without any runtime lookup table.

The rewrite system in Chapter 13 names its rules with \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}. A \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}P\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}>} carries the rule name as a compile-time string, allowing rewrite sets to be addressed and composed by name. The \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} factory (Chapter 13 and Chapter 14) follows the same pattern.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
template <FixedString Tag, typename Pred, typename Trans>
struct RewriteRule;
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} dispatch clauses of Chapter 8 use \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} indirectly through \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>}, but they also accept integral and enumerator keys. When a \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n} clause is keyed on a \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n}, the underlying \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} is what carries the regular-expression text into the type system.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
dsl::match(input,
  dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">>([](auto){ return "number"; }),
  dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"[a-z]+">>([](auto){ return "word"; }),
  dsl::otherwise([](auto){ return "other"; })
);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Finally, the PEG grammar layer of Chapter 27 and Chapter 28 uses \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} to name productions. The \texttt{a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} member and the \texttt{P\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}H\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}>} carrier both key on a \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}, so an entire PEG grammar can be spelled out in compile-time strings without a single runtime identifier table.

\section{Building Tagged Nodes}

The most common use of \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} is labelling AST nodes. Chapter 11 covers \texttt{l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f} and \texttt{n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} in depth; here we focus on the role of the tag. The tag is supplied as a string literal in angle brackets, and the compiler converts it to a \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} before the template is instantiated.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main ()
{
  auto x = dsl::leaf<"x">(1);
  auto y = dsl::leaf<"y">(2);
  auto add = dsl::node<"add">(x, y);
  std::cout << add.tag() << " " << add.dump() << "\n";
  // prints: add (add (x 1) (y 2))
}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The tag \texttt{"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}"} is a compile-time fact about the \texttt{n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} call. It cannot be changed at runtime, it cannot be supplied from a config file, and it cannot be built from user input. This rigidity is intentional: the structure of the tree is part of the program's design, not part of its runtime data.

Because the tag is part of the type, two \texttt{n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} calls with different tags instantiate different specializations of the \texttt{n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} template. The compiler is free to inline each specialization independently, and there is no virtual dispatch or string-table lookup involved in constructing a node. The only runtime work is the \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m} formatting of the value and the construction of the \texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e} itself.

The example program from \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} exercises this path end to end with only a handful of lines.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  auto n = dsl::leaf<"token">("identifier");
  auto t = dsl::node<"pair">(n, dsl::leaf<"kind">("name"));
  std::cout << t.tag() << " " << t.dump() << "\n";
}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Here \texttt{"\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}"} and \texttt{"\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}"} are leaf tags, and \texttt{"\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}"} is an inner-node tag. Each is a distinct \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} NTTP, and each flows through to the \texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e}'s label via \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}. The output is \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{})}, demonstrating that the compile-time tags have become ordinary runtime strings by the time the tree is dumped.

\section{Comparing Tags}

Because \texttt{o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}=} is defaulted and \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}, tags can be compared at compile time. This is useful whenever the structure of a tree is to be inspected by type rather than by value. A simple predicate over a node's tag can be written as a \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} function.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
template <dsl::FixedString Tag>
constexpr bool is_add (std::string_view t)
{
  return t == Tag.view ();
}

static_assert(is_add<"add">("add"));
static_assert(!is_add<"add">("sub"));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Two tags of the same length compare equal only when every byte matches, which is exactly the structural-equality rule the language applies to class-type NTTPs. This means that \texttt{i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} and a hypothetical second instantiation \texttt{i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} are the same specialization, and the compiler will not emit duplicate code for them.

Runtime comparison works just as well. The defaulted \texttt{o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}=} is callable on ordinary runtime objects, so a function that receives two \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} values (for example, by storing them in a container) can compare them with the usual \texttt{=\allowbreak{}=} syntax.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
bool same (dsl::FixedString<4> a, dsl::FixedString<4> b)
{
  return a == b;
}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Note that the two arguments must have the same \texttt{N}, because \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}4\allowbreak{}>} and \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}5\allowbreak{}>} are distinct types. When comparing tags of potentially different lengths, the correct approach is to compare their \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} results, since \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}=} handles differing lengths correctly.

The distinction between structural equality on the type and string equality on the view matters in two places. Inside the type system, two \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} arguments are the same template argument only when their bytes match exactly, so a \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} and a \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} (length three plus terminator) would be distinct specializations even though their visible text is the same. At runtime, by contrast, \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} collapses such distinctions because it compares only the visible characters. Most library code compares views, which is the more forgiving of the two.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
template <auto A, auto B>
constexpr bool same_tag ()
{
  return A.view() == B.view();
}

static_assert(same_tag<dsl::FixedString{"abc"}, dsl::FixedString{"abc"}>());
static_assert(!same_tag<dsl::FixedString{"abc"}, dsl::FixedString{"abcd"}>());
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\section{Tags as Dispatch Keys}

The \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} machinery described in Chapter 8 uses \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}-backed keys to build a compile-time dispatch table. Each \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n} clause carries its key as a template argument, and the \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h} implementation walks the clauses in order, comparing the input against each key. When the key is a \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>}, the comparison is a regular-expression match; when the key is a \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}-convertible value, the comparison is structural.

This pattern replaces the runtime \texttt{s\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h}-on-string idiom that C++ does not support natively. Instead of a \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}>}, the dispatch table is encoded directly in the type system, and the compiler is free to inline each branch.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto label = dsl::match("add",
  dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"add">>([](auto){ return "plus"; }),
  dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"sub">>([](auto){ return "minus"; }),
  dsl::otherwise([](auto){ return "unknown"; })
);
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The tags \texttt{"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}"} and \texttt{"\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}"} are \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} values embedded in the \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n} specializations. They never appear as runtime strings in the dispatch machinery itself; only their \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} results do, and only at the point where the matcher actually compares them against the input.

\section{A Tagged-Union-of-Values Demo}

A small but complete demonstration shows how \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} tags can drive a tagged-union style of value. Rather than declaring an enumeration and a variant, the tags are written directly into the type of each constructor. A visitor can then dispatch on the tag using \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>}.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main ()
{
  auto make_num = [](int v)  { return dsl::leaf<"num">(v); };
  auto make_str = [](std::string s) { return dsl::leaf<"str">(s); };

  auto v1 = make_num(42);
  auto v2 = make_str("hello");

  auto describe = [](auto const &node) {
    return dsl::match(node.tag(),
      dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"num">>([&](auto){ return "a number"; }),
      dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"str">>([&](auto){ return "a string"; }),
      dsl::otherwise([](auto){ return "something else"; })
    );
  };

  std::cout << describe(v1) << "\n";  // a number
  std::cout << describe(v2) << "\n";  // a string
}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The tags \texttt{"\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"} and \texttt{"\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}"} are compile-time facts about the two \texttt{l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f} calls. The \texttt{d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}e} function recovers them at runtime via \texttt{n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}, which returns the \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} that \texttt{l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f} stored from \texttt{T\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}. The \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} clauses then match against that view using the patterns, which are themselves \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}-backed.

This is the central pattern of MuDSL: compile-time names flow through \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} into runtime strings, and runtime strings are matched back against compile-time names. \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} is the hinge that makes the round trip possible.

\section{Limitations}

The first limitation is that \texttt{N} is part of the type. A \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}4\allowbreak{}>} and a \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}5\allowbreak{}>} are unrelated types, and there is no common base that holds a tag of arbitrary length. In practice this is invisible, because the constructor deduces \texttt{N} from the literal and the user never writes the length explicitly. It does mean, however, that a function cannot accept "any \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}" without making \texttt{N} a template parameter of its own.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
template <std::size_t N>
void process (dsl::FixedString<N> tag);   // accepts any length
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The second limitation is that the bytes are copied into the type. A \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}>} carries one hundred bytes in its \texttt{d\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a} array, and every copy of the value copies those bytes. For the short tags typical of a grammar this is negligible, but it does mean that \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} is not a good vehicle for large compile-time blobs. The type is designed for identifiers, not for payloads.

The third limitation is fundamental to the NTTP mechanism: the string must be a literal known at compile time. A \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} cannot be constructed from a \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}, from user input, or from any value that is not a constant expression. This is not a deficiency of \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}; it is the defining property of a non-type template parameter. Runtime-built strings must be carried by other means, such as the \texttt{A\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e}'s label or the payload of a \texttt{l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f}.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
std::string user_tag = read_from_config();
// dsl::leaf<user_tag>(42);   // error: user_tag is not a constant expression
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The fourth limitation follows from the structural-equality rule: two tags compare equal as NTTPs only if their bytes match exactly, including the terminator. Padding or trailing spaces will produce distinct template specializations. Users should therefore write tags as bare literals without extraneous whitespace.

\section{Relationship to Standard Proposals}

The C++ standardization committee has, over several cycles, considered a \texttt{f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} type that would formalize the pattern represented here by \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}. The most visible appearance of the idea in production code is \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t}'s use of a fixed-string type to carry format strings as compile-time constants, enabling compile-time validation of format specifiers. \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} is a minimal, self-contained realization of the same idea, tuned for the specific needs of an embedded-DSL library.

The standard proposals differ from \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} in detail — they may support trailing capacity, constexpr iteration, or integration with \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} — but the core principle is identical: a literal class type with a \texttt{c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r} array, usable as an NTTP, with \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} access to its contents. Should a standardized \texttt{f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} arrive, it would be a drop-in replacement for \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} at the template-argument position, and the rest of MuDSL would be unaffected.

Until then, \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} provides the same service with a ten-line definition and no dependencies beyond the standard library headers that \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} already includes. Its smallness is a feature: it makes the type easy to read, easy to reason about, and easy to verify against the structural-type rules of C++20.

\section{Summary}

\texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}>} is a literal class type whose single non-static data member is a \texttt{c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r} array of size \texttt{N}. Its constructor deduces \texttt{N} from a string literal via a reference-to-array parameter and copies the literal, terminator included, into the array with \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}n}. The \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} method returns a \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} of length \texttt{N\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}1}, and a defaulted \texttt{o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}=} provides structural equality. Every member is \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}, so the type is usable in any constant-expression context.

Because \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} is a literal class type with structural members, it qualifies as a C++20 class-type non-type template parameter. This is what allows string literals to appear inside angle brackets throughout MuDSL: \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>}, \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>}, \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>}, \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>}, \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>}, the keys of \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>}, and the \texttt{a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} calls of the PEG layer all depend on this property. The tag is a compile-time fact carried in the type; its \texttt{v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} is the runtime string recovered from it.

The type carries the usual limitations of an NTTP carrier: \texttt{N} is part of the type, the bytes are copied into the type, and only string literals — not runtime strings — may be used as arguments. Within those limits, \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} is the mechanism that lets MuDSL spell a grammar in source code rather than in configuration. The remaining chapters of this manual use it freely, and this chapter is the foundation for all of them.
